;Match details

* '''Match 1: Jusuf def. Tanaka.''' The truth-teller's final submission. Tanaka's brawling opened the block's last night; the mosaraza kneebar at 10:17 closed Jusuf's G1 with a fifth submission win. Jusuf finishes 6-3, 12 points — the block's quiet third, one point behind the leaders, the math never quite breaking his way. Tanaka finishes 1-8. '''Crowd:''' BOOS for Jusuf, respect applause for Tanaka. The Bulldog's G1 ends; the betrayal is a year old, and the floor is the floor.

* '''Match 2: Kuroki def. Reyes.''' The enforcer's close. Reyes, fresh off the biggest upset of the tournament, fought like a man with nothing left to prove; Kuroki's Black Iron lariat at 12:55 ended it. Kuroki finishes 6-3, 12 points — the block's quiet fourth, tied with Jusuf, the Kuroshio enforcer's G1 ending one point behind the leaders. Reyes finishes 1-8, 2 points — off the floor, and the Tsuda win over KAZUKI stands as the tournament's shock. '''Crowd:''' '''KUROKI''' chants, '''REYES''' chants behind them. The crowd's verdict on both men is warm.

* '''Match 3: Takagi def. Ono.''' The Dragon against the young ace, the block's last collision of generations. Ono's speed had Takagi turning; the Dragon lariat at 15:28 — the block's most decisive single move, one last time — ended it. Takagi finishes 7-2, 14 points — second in the block on tiebreakers, the Dragon's G1 ending two points behind the giant. Ono finishes 4-5, 8 points, the debut complete. '''Crowd:''' '''TAKAGI''' chants, '''ONO''' chants behind them. The passing of the guard waits another year, but the young man leaves the G1 having passed Ishida and stood with Takagi.

* '''Match 4: KAZUKI def. Diallo.''' The slide stops — too late. KAZUKI, three straight losses behind him, needed the win to salvage the tournament's dignity; the Twin Star Press at 14:36 delivered it. KAZUKI finishes 6-3, 12 points — tied with Jusuf and Kuroki, the block he led for five rounds finishing without him in the top two. Diallo finishes 2-7. '''Crowd:''' '''KAZUKI''' chants, mixed with boos from the section that remembers the Fall. The Shooting Star bowed to the crowd and walked out — the rebuild continues.

* '''Match 5: Taumafa def. Ishida.''' The main event, and the family closing the block. Ishida, the block's best story, took the Ishida Lock to the giant early; Taumafa's power was the answer, and the Samoan driver at 19:03 ended the resurrection one win short. Taumafa is 8-1, 16 points, the Block B winner — the family's second finalist in two years, and the Aizawa Dome on October 18 now has its other man. Ishida finishes 4-5, 8 points — the television champion's G1 ends with the block's best story and four submissions, including the man who ended the 1,095-day reign. '''Crowd:''' '''TAUMAFA''' chants, '''ISHIDA''' chants behind them. STANDING OVATION for both — the Toyozawa crowd knows what the block's final night meant. Taumafa stood in the ring and pointed to the sky — the family's gesture, the Fono's warrior, the Aizawa Dome waiting.

